    "footnotes": {
      "median": "The national median: half of the peer buildings use more energy, half use less. The reference data are mostly CBECS 2018; hospitals, medical offices, senior living, convenience stores and vehicle dealerships use industry surveys, multifamily a Fannie Mae survey.",
      "na": "Printed N/A (no reference data): parking, manufacturing/industrial plants and single-family homes. Data centers are printed as a PUE (1.82), not an EUI; water and wastewater plants as energy per flow. None of these has a kBtu/ft2 median here."
    }
  },
  "policy": {
    "note": "CAMBER screening policy, not part of the source and not a standard: the factors and bounds below turn one national median into a deliberately wide plausibility window for detecting a 1000x unit-prefix error. They are not a benchmark and say nothing about efficiency.",
    "total": {
      "plausible_low_factor": 8,
      "plausible_high_factor": 6,
      "implausible_low_factor": 60,
      "implausible_high_factor": 25
    },
    "single_fuel": {
      "plausible_high_factor": 6,
      "implausible_high_factor": 25
    },
    "hard": {
      "electricity": {"implausible_below": 0.1, "implausible_above": 10000},
      "district_chilled_water": {"implausible_above": 10000},
      "thermal": {"implausible_above": 3000},
      "total": {"implausible_below": 0.3, "implausible_above": 10000}
    },
    "generic_plausible": {
      "electricity": [1, 5000],
      "district_chilled_water": [0, 5000],
      "thermal": [0, 1500],
      "total": [5, 5000]
    },
    "basis": "The hard bounds hold for any building: 10,000 kBtu/ft2/yr of electricity is a continuous 330 W/ft2 over the gross floor area, above any data centre; 3,000 kBtu/ft2/yr of a heating fuel is a continuous 340 Btu/h/ft2, several times a laboratory's. A correct meter lies inside them, and the same meter read 1000x too high lies outside for any real EUI above 3 (thermal) or 10 (electricity) kBtu/ft2/yr."
  },
